      "publicationName": "Information Technology & Systems",
      "openaccess": "false",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-032-27541-7_40",
      "publisher": "Springer",
      "publisherName": "Springer Nature Switzerland",
      "publicationDate": "2027-01-01",
      "publicationType": "Book",
      "abstract": "Big Data analytics increasingly powers large-scale screening systems, from fraud detection to predictive policing and border security. These platforms promise efficiency by integrating heterogeneous databases, such as geolocation, biometrics, and social networks, but they also conceal a critical risk: coincidental matches grow explosively as comparisons multiply. We show that when multiple attributes are combined across many individuals, the probability of at least one false alert can rise in a double-exponential manner. Unlike classical multiple-comparisons problems, these systems perform silent tests at massive scale without p-values or transparency, making standard error control inapplicable. We develop exact formulas and approximations to model system-level risks, and while we use AI surveillance as a case study, our framework generalizes to any Big Data screening platform where rare coincidences can overwhelm decision-making. Our contribution is a unified probabilistic framework that quantifies when large-scale screening systems undergo a sharp transition from reliable to unreliable behavior due to double-exponential false alert amplification.",
